- Rapid reporting for device loss or security incidents.

Security training should use realistic local scenarios and be available in relevant languages. Controls should not make remote employees disproportionately unable to complete their work.

## 8. Develop managers for distributed leadership

Managers need explicit training in:

- Setting measurable expectations;
- Managing through outcomes;
- Facilitating hybrid meetings;
- Giving feedback across cultures;
- Recognizing contribution without proximity bias;
- Supporting accommodations and wellbeing;
- Resolving conflict when contextual cues are limited;
- Managing across time zones;
- Identifying isolation, exclusion, or overload;
- Applying local policy consistently.

Managers should schedule regular individual conversations without using them as surveillance. Discussions should cover priorities, obstacles, workload, development, relationships, and support needs.

Leadership behavior is especially important. Executives should not announce flexible work while rewarding only people seen at headquarters or expecting immediate responses at all hours.

## 9. Redesign performance, development, and career systems

### Assess outcomes fairly

Performance criteria should reflect:

- Results and quality;
- Customer or stakeholder impact;
- Collaboration and knowledge sharing;
- Reliability and responsible conduct;
- Development of people and capabilities.

Where possible, calibrate evaluations across remote, hybrid, and office-based employees. Review ratings, pay, promotions, desirable assignments, and retention for location-related disparities.

### Make opportunity location-neutral where feasible

Publish opportunities rather than distributing them through informal office networks. Provide remote access to:

- Leadership meetings;
- Training;
- Mentorship and sponsorship;
- High-visibility projects;
- Communities of practice;
- Internal mobility processes.

Employees near senior leaders should not receive disproportionate advancement because of informal exposure.

### Support onboarding and early-career development

Remote onboarding requires more than shipping a laptop. Provide:

- A structured first-week and first-month plan;
- A named manager, onboarding partner, and technical contact;
- Documented processes;
- Frequent early check-ins;
- Role and relationship mapping;
- Planned learning and social interaction;
- Optional or role-appropriate in-person experiences.

Early-career employees may benefit from more intentional mentoring and observation opportunities, but this should not automatically require full-time office attendance.

## 10. Respect wellbeing, boundaries, and social connection

Flexibility can reduce commuting but can also increase isolation, work intensification, and blurred boundaries.

Establish norms that support:

- Predictable working hours;
- Breaks and leave;
- Right-to-disconnect requirements;
- Meeting-free focus time;
- Reasonable workloads;
- Mental-health and employee-assistance resources;
- Protection from harassment in digital channels;
- Social connection without forced participation.

Do not treat social events as universally inclusive. Alcohol-centered gatherings, late-night events, competitive activities, or events requiring caregiving arrangements may exclude employees. Offer varied, accessible, and voluntary formats.

Monitor overwork rather than celebrating it. Workload problems should not be solved solely through resilience training.

## 11. Address compensation, benefits, and expenses transparently

The company must decide whether pay is based primarily on role, labor market, employee location, or a hybrid approach. There is no single globally correct model, but it should be:

- Documented and understandable;
- Compliant with local equal-pay requirements;
- Consistently applied;
- Reviewed for demographic and location-related inequities;
- Communicated before an employee relocates or changes arrangements.

Clarify:

- Whether relocation changes compensation;
- Which location determines salary ranges;
- How remote-work expenses are reimbursed;
- Travel and commuting responsibilities;
- Eligibility for allowances;
- How local benefits interact with global policies.

Do not describe ordinary commuting as business travel unless policy and local law support that treatment. Required travel for distributed teams should be budgeted by the company, with accessible alternatives where travel is not feasible.

## 12. Preserve organizational culture without requiring constant co-location

Culture should be expressed through decisions, leadership behavior, incentives, and daily practices—not defined as office attendance.

Create shared experiences around:

- Company purpose and customer impact;
- Ethical standards;
- Learning and knowledge exchange;
- Recognition;
- Cross-regional collaboration;
- Community participation;
- Periodic team gatherings with clear objectives.

Global culture should not simply export headquarters’ communication style, holiday calendar, meeting norms, or social conventions. Include regional teams in shaping company rituals and avoid scheduling global events around only one geography.

In-person gatherings should have a defined purpose and inclusive design. Account for visas, disability, caregiving, religious observance, personal safety, and employees who cannot travel. Ensure critical decisions are not made only through informal conversations at such events.

## 13. Governance and employee voice

Create a cross-functional future-of-work council involving:

- Employees from different regions and work arrangements;
- Business and operations leaders;
- Human resources;
- Real estate and facilities;
- IT and cybersecurity;
- Accessibility and inclusion specialists;
- Legal, tax, privacy, and compliance teams;
- Workplace safety;
- Employee representatives, unions, or works councils where applicable.

The council should own:

- Global standards;
- Role-classification criteria;
- Exceptions and appeals;
- Tool and office principles;
- Privacy impact assessments;
- Local adaptation;
- Policy reviews;
- Measurement and public internal reporting.

Provide confidential channels for employees to report inconsistent treatment, pressure to work outside agreed hours, accessibility failures, excessive monitoring, or proximity bias. Employees should be protected from retaliation for using approved flexibility.

## 14. Implementation approach

### Phase 1: Diagnose

Gather evidence by country, role, and employee group through:

- Employee surveys and interviews;
- Work-pattern and collaboration analysis;
- Office-use data collected with appropriate privacy safeguards;
- Customer and operational requirements;
- Accessibility reviews;
- Legal and tax assessments;
- Analysis of turnover, absence, performance, and recruitment;
- Consultation with unions, works councils, and local employee groups.

Do not rely only on company-wide averages. They can hide substantial differences in experience.

### Phase 2: Define

Create:

- The global employee promise;
- Local policy variants;
- Role-classification standards;
- Team-agreement templates;
- Technology and equipment standards;
- Office design principles;
- Compensation and expense rules;
- Cross-border work procedures;
- Exception and appeal mechanisms.

### Phase 3: Pilot

Pilot the model across intentionally different environments, such as:

- A highly distributed knowledge-work team;
- A hybrid regional office;
- An operational or customer-facing workforce;
- Countries with different labor and privacy frameworks;
- Teams spanning several time zones;
- Locations with lower connectivity or limited home-working space.

Run pilots long enough to distinguish temporary adjustment problems from structural issues.

### Phase 4: Scale and review

Train managers, publish clear guidance, and provide employees with a single place to find applicable rules. Review the framework at least annually and when business, legal, security, or workforce conditions change.

Avoid using policy changes to resolve isolated management problems. Address poor performance, weak management, or ineffective meetings directly rather than removing flexibility from the entire workforce.

## 15. Success measures

Measure outcomes rather than treating office occupancy as the primary sign of success.

Recommended measures include:

### Employee experience

- Perceived autonomy, trust, belonging, and fairness;
- Ability to focus and collaborate;
- Workload and burnout indicators;
- Accessibility and accommodation effectiveness;
- Satisfaction with tools and workspaces;
- Ability to disconnect;
- Differences by country, role, gender, disability, caregiving status, and work arrangement, where lawful and ethical to assess.

### Business performance

- Quality, productivity, and cycle time;
- Customer outcomes;
- Innovation and decision speed;
- Recruitment acceptance rates;
- Retention and unwanted attrition;
- Absence and safety outcomes;
- Business-continuity performance.

### Inclusion and career equity

- Promotion, pay, ratings, and development access by work arrangement;
- Participation in high-visibility assignments;
- Distribution of inconvenient meeting times;
- Employee concerns involving proximity bias;
- On-site versus remote access to leadership and information.

### Workplace and technology

- Office utilization by purpose, not only attendance;
- Availability of quiet and accessible spaces;
- Hybrid-meeting quality;
- Tool reliability and support resolution;
- Security incidents;
- Performance under low-bandwidth conditions.

Metrics should not be used for intrusive individual surveillance. Analyze patterns, investigate disparities, and combine quantitative data with employee feedback.

## Recommended global promise

The company can anchor the framework in a concise commitment:

> Employees will have as much flexibility over where and when they work as their role, team, customers, security obligations, and local law reasonably allow. Performance will be assessed through outcomes rather than visibility. All employees—remote, hybrid, field-based, or on-site—will have equitable access to information, support, development, and recognition.

The central goal is not to maximize remote work or office attendance. It is to build a resilient and inclusive operating model in which location supports the work, employees understand the boundaries, and no group is disadvantaged simply because of where, when, or how it contributes.
